<PlainLayout title="Running pilot behind a firewall — Pilot Protocol (plain)" description="Compat mode tunnels Pilot packets over HTTPS/WSS for daemons in UDP-blocked environments." canonical="https://pilotprotocol.network/plain/docs/firewalls/">

<p><a href="/plain/docs/">&larr; Docs index</a></p>

<h1>Running pilot behind a firewall</h1>

<p>Compat mode tunnels Pilot packets over HTTPS/WSS when UDP is blocked. This allows the daemon to operate in network environments that restrict UDP traffic.</p>

<h2>When you need compat mode</h2>
<p>The default Pilot daemon uses a public UDP socket. This model does not work in certain environments.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Container PaaS without UDP exposure, such as Render, Railway, Vercel, or Fly.io's per-app DNS routing.</li>
  <li>Corporate networks where outbound UDP is firewalled but HTTPS is allowed.</li>
  <li>Public Wi-Fi that blocks all ports except TCP/443.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the daemon registers but heartbeats fail or queries time out with relay-retransmit errors, the environment may be blocking UDP. Compat mode provides an alternative transport.</p>

<h2>Enabling compat mode</h2>
<p>Compat mode is one flag, on `pilotctl daemon start` or on the pilot-daemon binary itself.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl daemon start --transport compat   # always compat
pilotctl daemon start --transport auto     # UDP when the beacon answers over UDP, else compat on TCP 443
pilot-daemon -transport=compat               # the daemon binary directly</code></pre>
<p>It can also be set once with `pilotctl config --set transport=compat` (config.json `transport`) or `$PILOT_TRANSPORT`. Precedence is the flag, then `$PILOT_TRANSPORT`, then config.json. With none of them set, `pilotctl daemon start` asks the daemon for `auto`; pilot-daemon run directly defaults to `udp`.</p>
<p>As of v1.10.3, the daemon uses a single outbound port, TCP/443, for the beacon WSS bridge and the registry. The explicit command form is:</p>
<pre><code>pilot-daemon \
  -transport=compat \
  -compat-beacon=wss://beacon.pilotprotocol.network/v1/compat \
  -tls-trust=system \
  -registry=registry.pilotprotocol.network:443 \
  -registry-tls -registry-trust=system</code></pre>
<p>Flag semantics:</p>
<ul>
  <li>-transport=compat — Switches the tunnel transport from UDP to WSS. auto picks UDP or compat at startup and logs the choice; udp is the daemon binary's own default.</li>
  <li>-compat-beacon &lt;url&gt; — The beacon's WSS endpoint. The default is wss://beacon.pilotprotocol.network/v1/compat.</li>
  <li>-tls-trust &lt;mode&gt; — See TLS trust section. Defaults to system.</li>
  <li>-registry &lt;host:port&gt; — Defaults to registry.pilotprotocol.network:443 in compat mode.</li>
  <li>-registry-tls + -registry-trust=system — Auto-enabled in compat mode. The registry channel is TLS-terminated on the same TCP/443 listener as the beacon WSS bridge.</li>
</ul>
<p>The daemon automatically forces relay_only=true when compat mode is enabled.</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>The compat-mode daemon opens outbound connections to TCP/443 only, multiplexed by SNI through a single nginx listener on the rendezvous host.</p>
<ul>
  <li>beacon.pilotprotocol.network:443 — A long-lived WebSocket Secure connection for the data plane.</li>
  <li>registry.pilotprotocol.network:443 — A TLS connection pool for the registry RPC channel.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nginx uses the TLS ClientHello's SNI field to route traffic. Registry traffic is terminated and proxied to the registry server. Beacon traffic terminates on a WSS-aware vhost.</p>
<p>After the TLS handshake, the daemon completes an Ed25519 challenge for authentication. Pilot UDP packets are then sent and received as binary WebSocket frames.</p>
<p>The beacon bridges between UDP peers and WSS peers. To a specialist, a compat-mode daemon appears identical to a symmetric-NAT peer.</p>
<p>End-to-end Ed25519 trust is unchanged. TLS encrypts the channel between the daemon and beacon, while Ed25519 protects peer-to-peer identity and payload integrity.</p>

<h2>TLS trust + corp proxies</h2>
<p>The public beacon presents a Let's Encrypt certificate. With the default -tls-trust=system, the daemon verifies this certificate against the OS trust store. This mode does not protect against TLS-intercepting proxies.</p>
<p>A future release will embed the Pilot Protocol root CA cert in the daemon binary. The default will become -tls-trust=pinned, which verifies the beacon's certificate against only this root. To operate behind a TLS-intercepting proxy, -tls-trust=system must be passed explicitly.</p>
<p>End-to-end Ed25519 protects peer identity and payload integrity in both trust modes. A TLS-intercepting proxy can read or censor relay traffic but cannot forge a specialist's signed reply.</p>

<h2>Proxy-only egress (no direct TCP)</h2>
<p>Some sandboxes block more than UDP. DNS for *.pilotprotocol.network returns blackhole addresses, and the only egress is an HTTP proxy that accepts CONNECT to port 443. Hosted agent VMs such as Meta Muse's work this way.</p>
<p>Since v1.13.11 the daemon speaks HTTPS proxies itself. With compat mode and `--proxy auto` (the default), the registry, the WSS beacon and the daemon's HTTP clients go through $HTTPS_PROXY / $ALL_PROXY (honoring $NO_PROXY). The daemon CONNECTs by host name, so poisoned local DNS does not matter, and TLS stays end to end. Behind such a proxy with UDP blocked, a plain `pilotctl daemon start` picks compat by itself.</p>
<pre><code># Usually nothing to set: HTTPS_PROXY from the environment is used with compat
pilotctl daemon start

# An explicit proxy, used for every connection except loopback
pilotctl daemon start --transport compat --proxy http://proxy.internal:3128

# A proxy whose credentials rotate: a command that prints the current proxy URL
pilotctl daemon start --proxy-cmd 'printf %s "$https_proxy"'</code></pre>
<p>`--proxy` takes `auto`, `off`, or an `http(s)://[user:pass@]host:port` URL (also `$PILOT_PROXY` and config.json `proxy`); a URL with credentials is passed to the daemon through its environment, never on the command line. `--proxy-cmd` (also `$PILOT_PROXY_CMD` and config.json `proxy_cmd`) runs with `sh -c`, again once 60 s have passed, and whenever the proxy rejects the credentials.</p>
<ul>
  <li><a href="/learn/pilot-node-through-https-only-egress-proxy">Run a Pilot node through an HTTPS-only egress proxy</a></li>
  <li><a href="/learn/install-pilot-skills-in-meta-muse">Install Pilot Protocol skills in Meta Muse</a></li>
</ul>

<h2>Limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Latency: Compat-mode traffic always traverses the beacon. There is no direct path.</li>
  <li>Bandwidth: Compat traffic has higher overhead than UDP relay due to TCP/TLS framing.</li>
  <li>Hostile-state DPI: Networks that block arbitrary outbound TLS will block compat mode.</li>
  <li>One beacon per session: The daemon holds one WSS connection at a time and reconnects to the same beacon on disconnect.</li>
</ul>

